Subject: [PATCH 03294/11362] [outer-loop] arc: global-budget competition
 dev-harness + live-runtime architecture

Operator vision (2026-06-21): the rate limit (10 steps/sec -> ~288k real steps / 8h play cap; 12h is the
Kaggle NOTEBOOK cap, 2026 play-cap UNCONFIRMED) is the bottleneck. Build a system that runs the entire
budget, allocates every API call usefully, learns each game on the fly via free internal simulation, banks
per-level success, gives up + banks saved budget, remembers attempts, in a continuous instrumented dev loop.

Built the FOUNDATION (the substrate everything else iterates in):
- scripts/arc_compete_sim.py: global-budget dev-harness. Runs the agent stack against the offline arcade
  (a LOCAL API simulator over environment_files) under a parameterized total step budget, with DYNAMIC
  per-game allocation (remaining // remaining_games -> early wins BANK budget for later games), per-game
  give-up, full per-game + global instrumentation (cap/used/saved/levels/efficiency/give-up/utilization),
  and the AUTHORITATIVE EnvironmentScoreCalculator (via arc_leaderboard_eval.run_game) so local can't drift
  from the leaderboard. Validated end-to-end.
- Baseline it establishes: the bare explorer scores 0.0 (0 levels, ~98% budget burned) on
  ar25/ka59/cd82/sp80 -- the generalization wall, now a measurable dev-loop number to drive up.

docs/research-notes/arc-live-competition-architecture-2026-06-21.md organizes the full vision into 8
composable engines (sim / pacer-scheduler / learned-world-model / planner / per-level-feedback /
cross-attempt-memory / meta-strategy / instrumentation), their status, and the build roadmap. NEXT: the
neural learned-dynamics backend (the make-or-break learn-from-play test), then wire it into the harness and
watch the 0.0 baseline move.

